WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.300
To catch these blind spots, institutions
rely on research ethics committees as

2
00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:07.260
their primary oversight mechanism.

3
00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:11.600
These committees use a formal, five-step
framework to strip away bias from

4
00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:16.360
complex protocols. This process moves from
defining actual versus perceived

5
00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:21.020
conflicts to applying hard management
plans, such as recusal or informational

6
00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:22.660
firewalls between research teams.

7
00:00:22.700 --> 00:00:26.920
Ethical oversight isn't a one-time
checklist at the start of a grant.

8
00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:30.560
It requires a continuous pressure test
maintained throughout the entire life

9
00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:35.460
of the project. Moving from identification
to resolution requires executing

10
00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:38.280
structural best practices at the
institutional level.

11
00:00:38.740 --> 00:00:42.880
For emerging technologies, organizations
should establish independent AI and

12
00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:46.000
ethics review boards that are strictly
separated from product development

13
00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:49.760
teams. Institutions must also implement
whistleblower protections.

14
00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:54.280
This ensures researchers can voice ethical
dissent without fear of academic or

15
00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:59.540
corporate retaliation. The ultimate fail-
sace is the contractual exit option.

16
00:00:59.780 --> 00:01:02.980
Researchers should hold the legal right to
withdraw their work if they

17
00:01:03.020 --> 00:01:06.620
discover their findings are being used to
violate ethical norms.

18
00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:11.000
Transparency and disclosure function as
the primary immune system for modern

19
00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:14.940
research, protecting the core integrity of
the scientific method and the

20
00:01:14.980 --> 00:01:16.104
safety of the public.
